    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We start with the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\). To factor this expression, we need to find the greatest common factor (GCF) of the terms \\(9x^2\\) and \\(3x\\).\n\n1. Identify the GCF of the coefficients 9 and 3. The GCF of 9 and 3 is 3.\n2. Identify the GCF of the variable parts \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\). The GCF of \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\) is \\(x\\).\n\nThus, the GCF of the entire expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is \\(3x\\).\n\nNext, we factor out the GCF \\(3x\\) from each term in the expression:\n\n\\[\n9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1) = 3x(3x + 1)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the completely factored form of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n\\]",
